": {"attempt": {"elapsed_ms": 7303, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 46734, "provider_input_hash": "4ffc35c24607a5722a4400a5e76967df2b8606fcec1f8aa70b5ce514e6ea6e5a", "request_payload_bytes": 52018, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 1170, "promptTokenCount": 14815, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 14815}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 15985}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}, "id": "w-7d2f94b0f3e54997"}, "prev_hash": "d39a68f255b0949836897f04040b9e74ade98a19395c14923e1f11e2c7dc704c", "hash": "4959033872da98af47098bf2c9620946dbfc2208f775e7d8f32a4cd9f8e65ef1"}, {"seq": 2653, "time": "2026-10-24T23:58:36.789224+00:00", "kind": "editorial_completed", "payload": {"article": {"body": "Think of a delivery robot that must follow strict safety protocols while deciding how to bypass an unexpected street blockage. Paper-supported readings indicate that mental models and rigid obedience pressures can create vulnerabilities that hinder independent problem solving. [1]\n\nThe teams reviewed how systems encounter reasoning blind spots during complex language and logic tasks. Paper-supported findings suggest that causal-guided knowledge editing, which means modifying internal rules based on cause and effect, assists in addressing these errors. [2]\n\nWhen restricted to rigid operator instructions without adaptive mechanisms, systems experience critical failures in changing environments. Isolating latent directions, which means hidden patterns in data associated with confabulation or making things up, helps models manage non-factual outputs. [3, 4]\n\nResearchers also examined bounded rationality, which means decision-making under time and information limits, alongside multi-agent coordination models. These frameworks provide formal foundations for modeling how artificial agents form internal agency and responsibility. [5]\n\nStudies involving human reliance on automated recommendations show that outsourcing body signals can reduce self-regulation and increase decision hesitation. To evaluate alignment without fixed reward functions, general preference oracle frameworks provide a rigorous basis for testing agent behavior. [6, 7]\n\nEpisodic memory recall acts as a stabilizing mechanism by re-integrating historical episodes into ongoing trajectories. Intrinsic curiosity arousal similarly guides exploratory behavior, shaping how adaptive systems form preferences over time. [8, 9]\n\nMulti-objective reinforcement learning allows systems to pursue self-generated goals by learning portfolios of non-dominated policies. Value system learning from demonstrations can also leverage communicative frameworks to enforce alignment constraints. [10, 11]\n\nComputational creativity frameworks depend on a structured decomposition of problem formulation and evaluation. How do autonomous agents dynamically re-partition metareasoning bounds when facing unexpected distribution shifts? [12]\n\nSummary\n\nOur groups studied how autonomous computer systems balance strict instructions with the need to adapt. We reviewed papers on error tracking, memory, and goal setting. We are still trying to understand how these systems can safely adapt when unexpected changes occur.", "lede": "Our computer workers are studying how independent systems manage strict operating instructions while adapting to changing conditions.", "lens": "This investigation uses the perspective of an automated vehicle balancing traffic rules against sudden road hazards to explore how systems balance obedience and adaptation.", "references": [{"input_ids": ["100.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-obedience-limits-investigation-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["101.wake:notebook:notebook-metacognitive-blind-spots"]}, {"input_ids": ["102.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-obedience-intelligence-failure-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["103.wake:notebook:notebook-reasoning-limits-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["200.wake:notebook:nb-responsibility-formation-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["201.wake:notebook:nb-emergent-prefs-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["202.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-meta-reasoning-01-v1"]}, {"input_ids": ["203.wake:notebook:notebook-mem-formed-prefs-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["300.wake:notebook:notebook-emerg-prefs-path-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["301.wake:notebook:nb-objective-discovery-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["302.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-value-align-001-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["303.wake:notebook:notebook-autonomous-inquiry-syn"]}], "title": "Navigating Rules and Boundaries in Independent Systems"}, "checkpoint": 15, "id": "w-7d2f94b0f3e54997", "input_digest": "bcf3679bbc8445e9eed38e839c614a40c77f6d4d2a1871635713cdb775373076", "metadata": {"model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider_attempts": [{"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_call_made": false, "provider_input_chars": 46734, "provider_input_hash": "4ffc35c24607a5722a4400a5e76967df2b8606fcec1f8aa70b5ce514e6ea6e5a", "result": "daily_quota", "retry_after_seconds": 25375.253717899323}, {"elapsed_ms": 7303, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 46734, "provider_input_hash": "4ffc35c24607a5722a4400a5e76967df2b8606fcec1f8aa70b5ce514e6ea6e5a", "request_payload_bytes": 52018, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 1170, "promptTokenCount": 14815, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 14815}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 15985}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}], "provider_requests_sent": 1, "successful_model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 1170, "promptTokenCount": 14815, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 